<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>

    <PackageId>Pandatech.SharedKernel</PackageId>
    <Authors>Pandatech</Authors>
    <Description>Opinionated ASP.NET Core 10 infrastructure kernel: OpenAPI (Swagger + Scalar), Serilog, MediatR, FluentValidation, CORS, SignalR, OpenTelemetry, health checks, maintenance mode, resilience pipelines, and shared utilities.</Description>
    <PackageTags>aspnetcore;serilog;mediatr;fluentvalidation;openapi;swagger;scalar;opentelemetry;signalr;healthchecks;resilience;shared-kernel;pandatech</PackageTags>

    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/PandaTechAM/be-lib-sharedkernel</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/PandaTechAM/be-lib-sharedkernel</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>

    <PackageIcon>pandatech.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

    <Version>2.4.6</Version>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>Picks up FluentImporter 6.2.0.

FluentImporter 6.2.0 reorders the generated import-template legend to lead with the allowed-value tables instead of
the column reference table, and stops appending a dropdown label that merely repeats its value, so a value set whose
values are already words renders "Yes" rather than "Yes - Yes". Files generated by either version import identically.

Previous release notes follow.

2.4.5 &#8212; Picks up FluentImporter 6.1.0 and FileExporter 8.1.0.

FluentImporter 6.1.0 adds WithExampleText, which declares the exact text a generated sample cell should carry. It is
what lets a column converted from text into a collection be marked required: an example typed to the destination
property was rendered as its type name, so the generated template failed its own parse on the blank cell.

FileExporter 8.1.0 writes booleans as the text Yes/No instead of as logical cells, which used to render in the
viewer's own Excel UI language and disagreed with CSV, and lets a declared HasFormat win over the property's CLR
type, which had made HasFormat(Date) dead on a DateTime and HasFormat(Integer) dead on a decimal. No public API break
in either.

Previous release notes follow.

2.4.4 — Picks up FileExporter 8.0.0, which adds ExportOptions for per-request overrides — localized column headers, an enum label resolver, and explicit file and sheet names — none of which were reachable before, because an ExportRule is a boot-time singleton. It also stamps the file name's timestamp at export time instead of at rule construction, raises the name cap from 30 to 100 characters, substitutes WithName's {DateTime} placeholder instead of shipping it as a literal, rejects an undefined export format explicitly, stops rendering "7 - " for undefined enum values, and makes CSV percentages agree with XLSX. No public API break.</PackageReleaseNotes>

    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

    <RunAnalyzersDuringBuild>false</RunAnalyzersDuringBuild>
    <RunAnalyzersDuringLiveAnalysis>true</RunAnalyzersDuringLiveAnalysis>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>false</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

